// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
; specs/widgets/fits-on.t27 -- how many copies of a design fit on an XC7A200T, and what runs out first
; Source of truth for public/widgets/fits-on/ (gHashTag/trinity, apps/website). The page is written
; by scripts/widget-pages-from-spec.mjs from this file and hands every constant below to tool.js as
; window.T27_WIDGET. tool.js holds no English of its own: every word on screen is a SAY_ constant
; here, every number the tool relies on is a K_ constant or a field of device.json.
; ASCII only (L3), English only (LANG-EN).

; WHAT IT DOES: the reader types the LUT count of a design, and if they like its flip-flop, DSP48E1,
; RAMB36E1 and RAMB18E1 counts. For each resource the page divides the XC7A200T's total by the
; count per copy and rounds down; the smallest quotient is the number of copies that fit, and the
; resource that gives it is named as the binding one. A grid draws one cell per copy over the
; binding resource's capacity, with what is left over after the last copy. Block RAM is counted in
; 18Kb halves: a RAMB36E1 takes two, a RAMB18E1 one, and the device has K_RAMB18E1 of them.

; HOW LUTS ARE DIVIDED: nextpnr-xilinx's SLICE_LUTX total, 269200, counts LUT BELs: 33650 slices
; times 8, a LUT6 BEL and a LUT5 BEL at each of the four positions A-D of a slice. A position holds
; one six-input LUT, or two smaller LUTs that share its inputs. In node0's routed result
; (/tmp/x7board/node0/node_routed.json) nextpnr placed its 4738 LUT cells on 4738 different
; positions: not one position was shared. So the page divides by positions, K_SLICE_LUTX / 2 =
; 134600, by default; the other button divides by the 269200 BELs, which is the ceiling only if
; every LUT found a partner, and is what nextpnr's own percent column divides by. No datasheet LUT
; figure is quoted: none was available to check locally.

; DATA PROVENANCE: device.json is written by scripts/widget-data/fits-on.mjs from the "Device
; utilisation" block of two real nextpnr-xilinx runs for the ALINX AX7203 board (part
; xc7a200tfbg484-2, chipdb xc7a200tfbg484-2.bin): /tmp/x7board/node0/nextpnr.log (top
; trinet_node_v2_ax7203, sha256 fbdeb0d27c53e0e0...) and
; ~/trinity-fpga/build/tnf16_board_ax7203/nextpnr.log (top tnf16_board_ax7203, sha256
; 60e4ef9b281d70f7...). Both logs print the same total for every BEL type; the script stops if
; they do not, and stops if a K_ number below is not the number it parsed. The logs carry no
; version line; the manifest.json beside each names nextpnr-xilinx 0.9.2-107-g7037c948. The
; example counts are the same blocks' used column and the yosys stat block of yosys.full.log
; beside each log (yosys 0.67+post b8e7da6f40, synth_xilinx -flatten -abc9 -nocarry -nodsp).

; WHAT IT DOES NOT CLAIM: that the copies route, meet timing or work. Every number is at 100% of
; the binding resource, an upper bound; no lower practical utilisation is applied, because none of
; this page's sources measures one. A yosys LUT count is a synthesis count, not the count nextpnr
; places. Pins, clocks, carry chains and routing are not counted. The PAD total is a chipdb BEL
; count, not a count of bonded pins; this page uses it for nothing and claims no pin count for the
; QMTech Wukong (fgg676) or any other package.
; phi^2 + 1/phi^2 = 3 | TRINITY

